{"context":"ACP (Agent Client Protocol) is the agent\u003c-\u003eclient bridge; v1 is stable and v2 is already published with a migration guide. v2 adds the primitives steward needs: `session/prompt.idempotencyKey` (dedupe), `session/resume` replay, `state_update.requires_action` (approval gating) and `$/cancel_request`/`tool_call/cancel`. ACP's centre of gravity is editor\u003c-\u003eagent; steward is a headless client. Claude Code and Codex do NOT speak ACP natively and travel through third-party adapters; native CLI JSON (`claude -p`, `codex exec --json`, `opencode run`) is a simpler fallback.","decision":"v1 baseline, v2-capable, version-AGNOSTIC client: negotiate the protocol major at `initialize` and support both side by side. Native CLI JSON is the PRIMARY transport; ACP is an adapter behind one interface. The SPIKE (`sto:acp-client-spike`) must negotiate v2 and exercise `requires_action`/`idempotencyKey`/`resume`/`cancel`, not merely probe v1. Permission semantics: the client MUST answer `session/request_permission` with a timeout=\u003edeny policy (never hang), keyed on normalized tool kind + rawInput (unknown =\u003e deny); `allow_always` disallowed by default; permission is ADVISORY and only meaningful paired with OS-level containment. A worker session carries a mandatory wall-clock deadline and resource model.","consequences":"The protocol is a replaceable adapter, not the core; a v2 migration is routine, not an ADR event. Per-session deadlines bound hung workers. Cost: adapters + policy engine to maintain; adapter-rot and third-party adapter fragility are accepted and re-probed.","alternativesConsidered":"Pin ACP v1 only (rejected: bets against a live v2 and forgoes the primitives the WIs need). ACP-only (rejected: Claude Code/Codex need third-party adapters). Bespoke protocol (rejected: reinvents ACP). ACP v2-only (rejected: v1 remains common; negotiate instead)."}